00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:06.760
So what is GraphQL? As a front end developer, you&nbsp;
often need to send requests to multiple services in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:12.560
order to fill your UI with data. For example,&nbsp;
you have issues of under fetching or need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.280
send requests to multiple services in order to&nbsp;
collect all the data you need. You might also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:24.000
be under fetching where you get too much data from&nbsp;
a single request. So you need to filter those out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.720
in your front end application. GraphQL solves this&nbsp;
problem by giving you one API that you can call to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:41.040
collect all the data you need. GraphQL is a query&nbsp;
language for APIs, much like how SQL is a query&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:49.080
language for databases. For example, the following&nbsp;
SQL statement where you would select a number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.080 --> 00:01:13.640
fields from a number of tables looks like this&nbsp;
in GraphQL. An operation, for example, query,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:20.080
which is for reading data and to find all the&nbsp;
fields that you want to retrieve, all in a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:26.640
request. So the way GraphQL is able to do this&nbsp;
is by having a type system. The type system is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:43.600
defined in a GraphQL schema. In a schema, you will&nbsp;
find all the available operations such as query&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:53.680
for reading data, mutations for adding, editing,&nbsp;
or deleting data or subscriptions to subscribe to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:02:06.440
updates in your data. The response for all these&nbsp;
operations are defined in your types. These types&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.440 --> 00:02:12.360
will have type definitions for all the fields that&nbsp;
are available in your schema. In order to get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:19.200
data from the GraphQL API you would need to take&nbsp;
your statement and pass it to the GraphQL API in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:32.440
document. This data is usually passed over HTTP&nbsp;
and returns data in JSON format. In the case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:37.400
where we want to connect multiple data sources,&nbsp;
the GraphQL API will make sure to collect the data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:45.480
from your underlying services. In this scenario&nbsp;
GraphQL could also serve as a middleware, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:52.120
we also call GraphQL Federation. In this video we&nbsp;
talked about GraphQL. I showed you how GraphQL can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:56.640
help you solve problems you might be facing as&nbsp;
a front end developer such as over fetching or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.760
under fetching. How to write the statements in&nbsp;
GraphQL and how GraphQL as the GraphQL schema&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:08.120
where all the time definitions are defined. And&nbsp;
finally, GraphQL can be used as a middleware to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:13.880
collect data from all your data sources. Thank&nbsp;
you. If you like this video and want to see more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:20.400
like it, please like and subscribe. If you have&nbsp;
questions, please drop them in the comments below.

